## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of module composition, workspace-based state, and simple CI gates in a beginner-friendly way. It ensures the candidate can wire a wrapper for consistent tagging across environments and validate constraints early in CI.

## Key Concepts

- Terraform modules and wrappers
- Variable validation for environment constraints
- Workspaces and workspace-aware backends
- Per-env state isolation with a single backend
- GitHub Actions gates around terraform plan

## Code Example

```javascript
// modules/env-wrapper/variables.tf
variable "environment" {
  type = string
  description = "Environment name (dev, prod)"
  validation {
    condition = contains(["dev","prod"], var.environment)
    error_message = "environment must be 'dev' or 'prod'"
  }
}
variable "cost_center" {
  type = string
}
```

```javascript
// modules/env-wrapper/main.tf
locals {
  common_tags = {
    Environment = var.environment
    CostCenter  = var.cost_center
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design cross-cloud multi-tenant infra from a single module, balancing reuse with isolation. Targets provider aliasing, per-tenant tagging, and policy-driven gates.

## Key Concepts

- Terraform provider aliasing and for_each
- Cross-cloud module composition
- Policy-as-code (OPA/Vault) for prod changes
- CI gates around per-tenant plan validation

## Code Example

```hcl
# skeleton: tenants map and module per-tenant
variable "tenants" {
  type = map(object({
    cloud  = string
    region = string
  }))
}

module "tenant" {
  for_each = var.tenants
  source   = "./modules/tenant"

  tenant_name = each.key
  cloud       = each.value.cloud
  region      = each.value.region

  # pass per-tenant providers (pseudo-syntax for illustration)
  providers = {
    aws    = aws.${each.value.cloud == "aws" ? "alias" : "default"}
    google = google.${each.value.cloud == "gcp" ? "alias" : "default"}
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test drift with cross-cloud resources and ensure idempotence? 
- What are trade-offs between centralized tenants state vs per-tenant backends?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a GitOps, multi-cloud per-PR sandbox architecture with isolated backends, secret management, and policy gates. It also probes automation for ephemeral environments and teardown reliability.

## Key Concepts
- Per-PR sandbox architecture across AWS, Azure, GCP
- Provider aliases per cloud and per PR
- Per-PR remote backends and state isolation
- Vault-backed credentials with rotation
- Encryption at rest and in transit
- Policy-as-code gates (OPA/Sentinel) in CI
- Teardown automation on PR lifecycle

## Code Example
```hcl
# Minimal scaffolding
terraform {
  required_providers {
    aws = { source = "hashicorp/aws" }
    azurerm = { source = "hashicorp/azurerm" }
    google = { source = "hashicorp/google" }
  }
}

provider "aws" {
  alias  = "pr1"
  region = "us-east-1"
}
provider "azurerm" {
  alias    = "pr1"
  features = {}
}
provider "google" {
  alias   = "pr1"
  project = "pr-12345"
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you implement per-PR teardown if resources are tainted or fail during apply?
- What strategies ensure Vault rotation never disrupts in-flight deployments while keeping state secure?

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses practical multi-cloud Terraform design, policy-as-code, drift handling, and test automation at scale—core for Oracle, Tesla, and LinkedIn-like environments.

## Key Concepts

- Provider aliasing for multi-region/multi-cloud setups
- Wrapper modules to standardize tagging and policy constraints
- Terraform Cloud/Sentinel or OPA for governance
- Drift detection workflows and isolated drift environments
- End-to-end testing with Terratest or kitchen-terraform

## Code Example

```hcl
# Wrapper module injecting standard tags
variable "environment" { type = string }
variable "cost_center" { type = string }
variable "tags" { type = map(string) }

module "service" {
  source = "../aws-azure-base"
  providers = {
    aws   = aws.us_east_1
    aws_eu = aws.eu_west_1
    azurerm = azurerm.westus2
  }
  tags = merge(var.tags, {
    Environment = var.environment
    CostCenter  = var.cost_center
  })
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you structure a Sentinel policy to enforce tag requirements and deny secrets in state?
- How would you simulate drift in tests and verify policy compliance across clouds?
